<%
/*
  Drop inside any public <form> that creates a record or sends mail. Pairs with
  captcha.service.js's requireHuman() on the matching route — one without the
  other is either a check nobody can pass or a form nobody checks.

  Two layers, deliberately:

  The honeypot needs no keys and no third party. Most abuse is a script filling
  every field it can find, and a field no human ever sees catches that outright.
  It is inside the form so the existing FormData -> Object.fromEntries submit
  path picks it up with no JS changes.

  Then whichever real check is available. With TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY set, Cloudflare
  Turnstile — stronger, and nearly invisible to a real person. Without it, the
  built-in challenge image, which the server generates and checks itself so that
  a deployment with no keys still has a working, visible check rather than a
  hidden field and a promise.
*/
%>
<div class="hidden" aria-hidden="true">
  <%
    /*
      Named "website" rather than something like "honeypot": a bot fills what
      looks fillable, so a plausible name catches far more than an honest one.
      tabindex="-1" keeps it out of keyboard navigation, and autocomplete="off"
      stops a browser helpfully filling it on a real person's behalf — which
      would lock them out of the form they are trying to use.
    */
  %>
  <label>Website
    <input type="text" name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" value="">
  </label>
</div>

<%
  /*
    `challenge: false` renders the honeypot and nothing else, for a form whose
    route runs requireHuman({ challenge: false }). The two have to agree: a
    visible box with no server check is theatre, and a server check with no box
    is a form nobody can submit.
  */
%>
<% if (locals.challenge === false) { %>
<% } else if (locals.turnstileSiteKey) { %>
  <div class="cf-turnstile my-3"
       data-sitekey="<%= turnstileSiteKey %>"
       data-response-field-name="captchaToken"
       data-theme="auto"></div>
<% } else { %>
  <%
    /*
      Rendered empty and filled in by /js/captcha.js, rather than server-side,
      so this partial works from any page without every page controller having
      to remember to issue a challenge. The image arrives a moment after the
      form; that is fine, because nobody submits before they have read it.
    */
  %>
  <div class="my-3" data-challenge>
    <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 mb-1" for="challenge-answer">
      Type the characters shown
    </label>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
      <div data-challenge-image
           class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md border border-gray-300 bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden"
           style="width:180px;height:60px"></div>
      <button type="button" data-challenge-refresh
              class="text-sm text-indigo-600 hover:underline px-2 py-2"
              title="Show different characters">New image</button>
    </div>
    <input id="challenge-answer" name="challengeAnswer" type="text"
           autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="characters" spellcheck="false"
           maxlength="8" required
           class="mt-2 w-40 rounded-lg border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-lg font-mono tracking-widest uppercase focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-indigo-500/40 focus:border-indigo-500">
    <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500">Not case sensitive. Click "New image" if you can't read it.</p>
  </div>
<% } %>
